# Decision Notes

Each decision records the problem, the options considered, what was chosen, why, and what it costs. Ordered from most to least important. The short version of these is in section 8 and 9 of `01-design-document.md`.

---

## D1. What each task returns

**Problem.** Results from many workers must be combined into one file holding the mean of every position. Each worker sees at most 5 files, so groups are always uneven (for example 12 files → groups of 5, 5 and 2).

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| A list of per-position averages | Intuitive | **Wrong** when groups have different sizes: small groups get too much weight |
| Averages + count | Correct with a weighted formula | Divides and re-multiplies at every step; more code and more rounding error |
| **A list of per-position sums + one count** ✅ | Correct; combining is plain addition; works in any order | Must store one extra number per item |

**Chosen:** each item is `{ sums: [C values, one per position], count: number of files }`. Combining adds lists index by index (position 1 with position 1, and so on) and adds counts. Each position is divided by the count once, in the final task.

**What it unlocks.**
- Any worker can combine any items, in any order.
- A raw file is simply an item with count = 1, so there is only one kind of combine task.
- No need to wait for "rounds" to finish.
- Built-in check: the final count must equal F.

**Why count is a single number.** Every file has all C positions, so the count is the same for every position. If files could have missing values, count would become a per-position list and the design would still work.

**Prior art.** The same principle as aggregation in federated learning: results are weighted by how much data they came from.

