## D2. How work is split across workers

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Static: give each worker F/W files at the start | No coordination | Job is as slow as the slowest worker (~1.7× ideal in simulation); breaks the 5-file limit |
| **Small tasks (≤ 5 inputs), workers pull one at a time** ✅ | Fast workers automatically do more; respects the limit | More tasks and messages |

**Why.** The assignment says workers run at different speeds and should idle as little as possible. With pulling (`prefetch = 1`), a slow worker simply does fewer tasks. Messages are tiny, so the extra count is cheap.

---

## D3. How partial results are combined

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| One reducer adds all partials at the end | Simple | Breaks the 5-file limit; one node handles ~20,000 partials on large jobs; single point of failure |
| Tree in fixed rounds (all of round 1, then round 2…) | Respects the limit | Fast workers idle at every round, waiting for the slowest task |
| **Basket: combine any ready items (up to 5), from any level** ✅ | Respects the limit; no waiting between rounds; no central bottleneck | Tree shape isn't known in advance |

**Why.** It's the only option that satisfies both the 5-file limit and "minimize idle time". The unpredictable tree shape doesn't matter, because the answer is identical for any shape (D1).

**Basket rules** (applied after every result and every scheduler tick):

1. ≥ 5 items ready → take 5, create a combine task.
2. ≥ 2 ready, nothing in flight, all files generated → take all, create the **final** task.
3. ≥ 2 ready and a worker is idle with the queue empty → take all, create a smaller task.
4. Otherwise → wait.

