## D4. Task size

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Always 2 inputs (binary tree) | Very small tasks | ~2× more tasks, deeper tree (17 levels vs 8 for 100k files) |
| Always exactly 5 | Fewest tasks | Workers can sit idle while 2–4 items wait |
| **Up to 5; fewer only when a worker would otherwise be idle** ✅ | Fewest tasks normally, no idling | Slightly more tasks near the end of a job |

**Why.** 5 is the largest the constraint allows, which minimizes tasks and tree depth. Allowing smaller tasks only when a worker is idle trades a few extra tasks for zero idle time.

---

## D5. Who decides what happens next

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Workers coordinate among themselves | No central component | Workers race to claim the same items; needs distributed locks; hard to debug |
| **One central scheduler** ✅ | All logic in one place; no races; workers stay simple | Must be restartable |

**Why.** Correctness is easiest when only one component changes the basket. All state is in PostgreSQL and results are acked only after the DB commit, so the scheduler can restart without losing anything. It only processes small messages, so it isn't a bottleneck. It is a second entry point of the TypeScript backend, not an extra system.

---

## D6. Queue technology

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **RabbitMQ** ✅ | `prefetch = 1` gives one task per worker natively; automatic redelivery when a worker dies; built-in dashboard | One more service |
| Redis Streams | Fast | Redelivery of a dead worker's tasks must be coded by hand |
| AWS SQS | Fully managed | Needs AWS; can't run offline |
| A table in PostgreSQL | No extra service | Polling; the assignment expects a separate queue |

**Why.** RabbitMQ gives fair one-at-a-time dispatch and crash recovery with no custom code, and runs locally in Docker.

---

## D7. Database

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **PostgreSQL** ✅ | Transactions; relational model fits jobs → tasks → items | Needs a schema |
| MongoDB | Flexible | Multi-document updates less natural |
| Redis only | Fast | Weaker durability; no relational queries |

**Why.** Processing one result changes several rows (task done, item added, next task created). A transaction makes this all-or-nothing, so a crash can never leave the basket half-updated.

---

## D8. How data moves between workers

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Numbers inside queue messages | No shared storage | Messages of many MB; queues are built for small messages |
| **Files in shared storage; messages carry only path + count** ✅ | Queues stay fast; files of any size | Storage must be reachable by all workers |

**Why.** One file can hold up to 999,999 values. Locally the storage is a shared folder; in production it would be S3 or MinIO.

---

## D9. File format

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| CSV | Human-readable | ~4× larger; slow to parse |
| **Binary NumPy `.npy`** ✅ | Compact; loads straight into arrays; values stay in position order | Not human-readable |

**Why.** Reading files is most of the work, so a compact format speeds up every task. The final result is also written as CSV for the user. Raw values are `float32`; sums and means are `float64` for precision.

---

## D10. Where files are generated

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| In the API | Simple | Large jobs (hundreds of GB) would freeze the API |
| **On workers, as GENERATE tasks (≤ 5 files each)** ✅ | Parallel; API stays responsive; combining starts while generation continues | Two task types |

**Why.** Generation is as heavy as the computation. Each file joins the basket as soon as it exists.

---

## D11. Fairness between jobs

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Enqueue all of a job's tasks immediately (FIFO) | Simple | A 100k-file job blocks every later job; cancelling leaves thousands of queued tasks |
| Shortest job first | Small jobs finish fastest | Large jobs can starve |
| **Shallow queue (~2×W waiting tasks), refilled round-robin across jobs** ✅ | Every job progresses; small jobs finish quickly; cancel and resize act immediately | More scheduler bookkeeping |

**Why.** The assignment requires accepting new jobs while others run. Round-robin is the simplest policy that's fair to all.

---

## D12. Duplicates and crashes

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Exactly-once delivery | Ideal | Not achievable in practice |
| **At-least-once delivery + idempotent processing** ✅ | Standard and reliable | A task occasionally runs twice |

**Why.** Three layers make a second run harmless: (1) results for tasks already `DONE` are ignored, (2) each output path is unique per task and enforced by a unique constraint, (3) the final count must equal F.

---

## D13. Detecting job completion

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Compare tasks done with a precomputed total | Easy to display | The total depends on runtime behaviour (D4), so it can't be known exactly |
| **Final task only when everything left is in it, nothing in flight, all files generated; then verify count = F** ✅ | Correct by construction, plus a safety check | None significant |

---

## D14. Changing the number of workers (bonus)

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| API runs `docker compose --scale` | Real containers | API needs Docker access (security risk) |
| **Worker manager process matching a desired count stored in the DB** ✅ | Simple; safe; same locally and on servers | Locally, workers share one container |

**Why.** Implements the bonus without giving the API control over Docker. Scaling down drains workers (finish current task, then exit). In production this maps to Kubernetes replicas.

---

## D15. Dashboard updates (bonus)

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **Polling every second** ✅ | Simplest | Up to 1 s delay |
| Server-Sent Events | Instant | A little more code |

**Why.** One second is fine for monitoring. The activity log can move to SSE later without changing anything else.

---

## D16. Who enqueues a new job

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Scheduler finds new jobs and enqueues them | All queue logic in one place | Doesn't match "the API should then enqueue the job"; up to 1 s delay |
| API enqueues all of a job's tasks | Matches the flow | Large jobs flood the queue (breaks D11) |
| **API enqueues the first batch; scheduler releases the rest** ✅ | Matches the flow; work starts immediately; fairness kept | Two components publish to the queue |

**Why.** Follows the assignment's flow while keeping the queue shallow and fair. If the API crashes after saving, unsent tasks stay `PENDING` and the scheduler sends them on its next tick.

---

## D17. Slow workers at the end of a job

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| Do nothing; rely on small tasks | Simplest | Simulation: small jobs up to 1.5× slower than fixed rounds |
| Never give tasks to slow workers near the end | Simple | Needs speed estimates; wastes capacity |
| **Backup copy: an idle worker duplicates a long-running task; first result wins** ✅ | Best result at every job size in simulation | A little duplicate work |

**Why.** The technique MapReduce uses for stragglers. It reuses the duplicate handling from D12.

**Simulation** (`bench/simulate.py`, 8 workers: 4 at 1×, 2 at 2× slower, 2 at 3× slower; ratio to theoretical best, lower is better):

| Files | Static split | Fixed rounds | Basket only | Basket + backups |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 20 | 2.98× | 2.06× | 3.43× | **2.06×** |
| 100 | 1.95× | 1.32× | 1.54× | **1.27×** |
| 1,000 | 1.73× | 1.10× | 1.05× | **1.02×** |
| 10,000 | 1.70× | 1.01× | 1.01× | **1.00×** |

